{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"goai-litsearch","tool":"save_to_library","arguments":{"papers_json":"[{"id":"W3204150380","title":"Monoclinic–orthorhombic first-order phase transition in K2ZnSi5O12 leucite analogue; transition mechanism and spontaneous strain analysis","authors":["A.M.T. Bell","Francis Clegg","C. M. B. Henderson"],"year":2021,"venue":"Mineralogical Magazine","doi":"10.1180/mgm.2021.67","arxiv_id":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1180/mgm.2021.67","pdf_url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/691FD3A78118E6441D1AAEEE9C709D1D/S0026461X21000670a.pdf/div-class-title-monoclinic-orthorhombic-first-order-phase-transition-in-k-span-class-sub-2-span-znsi-span-class-sub-5-span-o-span-class-sub-12-span-leucite-analogue-transition-mechanism-and-spontaneous-strain-analysis-div.pdf","abstract":"Abstract Hydrothermally synthesised K2ZnSi5O12 has a polymerised framework structure with the same topology as leucite (KAlSi2O6, tetragonal I41/a), which has two tetrahedrally coordinated Al3+ cations replaced by Zn2+ and Si4+. At 293 K it has a cation-ordered framework P21/c monoclinic structure with lattice parameters a = 13.1773(2) Å, b = 13.6106(2) Å, c = 13.0248(2) Å and β = 91.6981(9)°. This structure is isostructural with K2MgSi5O12, the first cation-ordered leucite analogue characterised. With increasing temperature, the P21/c structure transforms reversibly to cation-ordered framework orthorhombic Pbca. This transition takes place over the temperature range 848−863 K where both phases coexist; there is an ~1.2% increase in unit cell volume between 843 K (P21/c) and 868 K (Pbca), characteristic of a first-order, displacive, ferroelastic phase transition. Spontaneous strain analysis defines the symmetry- and non-symmetry related changes and shows that the mechanism is weakly first order; the two-phase region is consistent with the mechanism being a strain-related martensitic transition.","citation_count":7,"publication_type":"article","sources":["openalex"]},{"id":"W4386002389","title":"Structure and compositional trends in alkali-metal containing titanium and vanadium oxide chalcogenides and the new van der Waals phase Ti2Te2O","authors":["Nicola D. Kelly","Simon J. Clarke"],"year":2023,"venue":"Journal of Solid State Chemistry","doi":"10.1016/j.jssc.2023.124276","arxiv_id":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jssc.2023.124276","pdf_url":null,"abstract":"We report the solid-state synthesis of all eighteen layered oxide chalcogenides in the structural family AM2Q2 O (A = K/Rb/Cs; M = Ti/V; Q = S/Se/Te), allowing the determination of trends in composition and reactivity within the series. All materials are isostructural, crystallising in the primitive tetragonal space group P4/mmm as reported previously for five compounds in the series. The titanium or vanadium ions have intermediate valency on a single crystallographic site, leading to temperature-independent paramagnetism and correlated electronic behaviour which is influenced by the compositional variation. Furthermore, the alkali metal ions in and can be removed by oxidative deintercalation using at room temperature to produce a new metastable van der Waals layered phase, . During the deintercalation reaction the oxide chalcogenide layers undergo a relative shift by (0.5,0.5) in the ab plane such that is body-centered with space group I4/mmm.","citation_count":12,"publication_type":"article","sources":["openalex"]},{"id":"W3210703968","title":"Quasi-Zero Dimensional Halide Perovskite Derivates: Synthesis, Status, and Opportunity","authors":["Vanira Trifiletti","Ceyla Asker","Giorgio Tseberlidis","Stefania Riva","Keran Zhao","Weidong Tang","S. Binetti","Oliver Fenwick"],"year":2021,"venue":"Frontiers in Electronics","doi":"10.3389/felec.2021.758603","arxiv_id":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/felec.2021.758603","pdf_url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/felec.2021.758603/pdf","abstract":"In recent decades, many technological advances have been enabled by nanoscale phenomena, giving rise to the field of nanotechnology. In particular, unique optical and electronic phenomena occur on length scales less than 10 nanometres, which enable novel applications. Halide perovskites have been the focus of intense research on their optoelectronic properties and have demonstrated impressive performance in photovoltaic devices and later in other optoelectronic technologies, such as lasers and light-emitting diodes. The most studied crystalline form is the three-dimensional one, but, recently, the exploration of the low-dimensional derivatives has enabled new sub-classes of halide perovskite materials to emerge with distinct properties. In these materials, low-dimensional metal halide structures responsible for the electronic properties are separated and partially insulated from one another by the (typically organic) cations. Confinement occurs on a crystal lattice level, enabling bulk or thin-film materials that retain a degree of low-dimensional character. In particular, quasi-zero dimensional perovskite derivatives are proving to have distinct electronic, absorption, and photolu …[truncated]","citation_count":16,"publication_type":"article","sources":["openalex"],"abstract_truncated":true},{"id":"W4412488670","title":"High-Entropy Materials: A New Paradigm in the Design of Advanced Batteries","authors":["Yangmei Xin","Min Zhu","Haizhong Zhang","Xinghui Wang"],"year":2025,"venue":"Nano-Micro Letters","doi":"10.1007/s40820-025-01842-w","arxiv_id":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40820-025-01842-w","pdf_url":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s40820-025-01842-w.pdf","abstract":"High-entropy materials (HEMs) have attracted considerable research attention in battery applications due to exceptional properties such as remarkable structural stability, enhanced ionic conductivity, superior mechanical strength, and outstanding catalytic activity. These distinctive characteristics render HEMs highly suitable for various battery components, such as electrodes, electrolytes, and catalysts. This review systematically examines recent advances in the application of HEMs for energy storage, beginning with fundamental concepts, historical development, and key definitions. Three principal categories of HEMs, namely high-entropy alloys, high-entropy oxides, and high-entropy MXenes, are analyzed with a focus on electrochemical performance metrics such as specific capacity, energy density, cycling stability, and rate capability. The underlying mechanisms by which these materials enhance battery performance are elucidated in the discussion. Furthermore, the pivotal role of machine learning in accelerating the discovery and optimization of novel high-entropy battery materials is highlighted. The review concludes by outlining future research directions and potential breakthrou …[truncated]","citation_count":59,"publication_type":"article","sources":["openalex"],"abstract_truncated":true},{"id":"10.1007/978-981-19-6668-2_2","title":"Metastability in Thermodynamic Systems","authors":["Seiji Miyashita"],"year":2022,"venue":"Fundamental Theories of Physics","doi":"10.1007/978-981-19-6668-2_2","arxiv_id":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-19-6668-2_2","pdf_url":null,"abstract":null,"citation_count":0,"publication_type":"book-chapter","sources":["crossref"]},{"id":"10.46936/aps-194357/60017026","title":null,"authors":[],"year":2026,"venue":null,"doi":"10.46936/aps-194357/60017026","arxiv_id":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.46936/aps-194357/60017026","pdf_url":null,"abstract":null,"citation_count":null,"publication_type":"grant","sources":["crossref"]}]","library_path":"<HOME>"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{
